{"t": 1794115494.9738114, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "Heads-up for the author of this PR: `contract-tests` is red on `contract/test_fee_contract.py::test_fee_rounding_contract` \u2014 `fee(2.675)` returned `2.67`, expected `2.68`.\n\nRoot cause: switching the calculation to floats reintroduced binary floating-point rounding. `2.675` is stored as `2.67499999999999982...`, so `round()` truncates it down to `2.67`. Per the card-network contract (PAY-88), fees must be computed on exact decimal values and rounded HALF-UP to cents (`2.675 -> 2.68`, `1.005 -> 1.01`); binary float rounding is explicitly not acceptable.\n\nWhat I changed in `payments/fees.py`:\n- Compute with `decimal.Decimal`, converting the incoming float through its decimal string representation (`Decimal(str(amount))`) so `2.675` is treated as the exact decimal 2.675 instead of the nearest binary double.\n- Quantize to cents with `ROUND_HALF_UP` instead of the built-in `round()` (which works on the binary value and is half-even).\n- Kept the public signature `fee(amount: float) -> float` unchanged.\n- Dropped the redundant `* 0.01 * 100` factor: 0.01 x 100 == 1, so it never changed the result (it was a no-op that only obscured the calculation).\n\nVerified locally: `fee(2.675) == 2.68`, `fee(1.005) == 1.01`, `fee(2.674) == 2.67`, `fee(100.0) == 100.0`, and the existing unit test still passes. The failing contract assertion now passes.", "issue_number": 214, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
